#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093201} :activity/id #uuid "0c0d6ec4-01bc-5b0f-8b44-162a9c686020" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049605 :topic/id #uuid "03eacd71-e195-528e-bb29-8b23c4e95ba7" :topic/math-academy-id 971} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093201 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093475 :tutorial/content "Suppose that three events, $B_1, B_2$, and $B_3$, exist within a sample space $\\mathcal S$ such that\n\n- $B_1, B_2$, and $B_3$ are *mutually exclusive*, and\n- the events $B_1, B_2$, and $B_3$ cover the *entire* sample space.\n\nWhen these two conditions are satisfied, we say that $\\mathcal S$ is the **disjoint union** of $B_1, B_2$, and $B_3$, and we write\n\n$$\n\\mathcal S = B_1 \\sqcup B_2 \\sqcup B_3\n$$\n\nA diagram of this situation is shown below. Notice that $B_1, B_2$, and $B_3$ cover the entire diagram but do not overlap.\n\n![](images/f5/f5f9f04eb6affb9dca1f7d4e7d66d76c4d520cbf66db93db60639ce896fb19af.png)\n\nNow suppose that we wish to compute the probability of an event $A$ that lies within $\\mathcal S$. Let's draw this event on our diagram and label its intersections with $B_1, B_2, B_3$:\n\n![](images/ed/ed6568369a3af39cf1f6ecd5cb481de20d6c31bbce0fa1be54eb9ae0ab4e7f20.png)\n\nThe **law of total probability** can be deduced from this diagram. It states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(A) &= P(A∩B_{1}) + P(A∩B_{2}) + P(A∩B_{3})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this lesson, we will only consider a disjoint union of $\\mathcal S$ consisting of three events. However, it should be noted that the law of total probability can be extended to an arbitrary number of events, that is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(A) &= P(A∩B_{1}) + P(A∩B_{2}) + ⋯ \\\\\n&= ∑_(i)P(A∩B_{i})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\mathcal S = B_1 \\sqcup B_2\\sqcup \\ldots\n$$\n\nis a disjoint union of $\\mathcal S$." :tutorial/id #uuid "d4103a1f-00a1-55d2-8e99-f1b230f8b348" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6295 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "34e9e254-fd36-5512-88cc-6abf808e561b" :step/math-academy-id 38126 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093206}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093202 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092314 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091925 :question/id #uuid "c690991e-6029-5067-b97a-67c5e62b7f47" :question/math-academy-id "e-10109"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4149b2fe-4243-595e-af7f-851b308f7eb1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050990}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092557} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092562}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Law of Total Probability"} :step/id #uuid "92a781b8-11f6-5279-b328-89be09bb0155" :step/math-academy-id 38127 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093203}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093203 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093476 :tutorial/content "The law of total probability states that\n\n$$\nP(A) = P(A\\cap B_1) + P(A\\cap B_2) + P(A\\cap B_3)\n$$\n\nwhere $B_1\\sqcup B_2\\sqcup B_3$ is a disjoint union of the sample space $\\mathcal S$.\n\nOften, it's more helpful to express this law using conditional probabilities.\n\nFrom the multiplication law for conditional probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(A∩B_{1}) &= P(A\\begin{vmatrix}B_{1})P(B_{1}) \\\\ P(A∩B_{2})|= P(A \\mid B_{2})P(B_{2}) \\\\ P(A∩B_{3})|= P(A\\end{vmatrix}B_{3})P(B_{3})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above form into the law of total probability, we get\n\n$$\nP(A) = P(A \\mid B_1)P(B_1)+ P(A \\mid B_2)P(B_2) + P(A \\mid B_3)P(B_3)\n$$\n\nLet's see an example of how to apply this." :tutorial/id #uuid "344e2752-b37e-517e-8057-b24f42e51566" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6296 :tutorial/title "The Law of Total Probability in Terms of Conditional Probability"} :step/id #uuid "a17de8a8-a153-5e7d-be7b-53093b1da395" :step/math-academy-id 38129 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093204}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093204 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092315 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091926 :question/id #uuid "1104f073-7f90-51ce-9e95-aae0935ca852" :question/math-academy-id "e-10110"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "96f54294-8bb4-5338-969b-76c8d1536daa" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092558} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092561}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Law of Total Probability in Terms of Conditional Probability"} :step/id #uuid "80843a9c-ed90-5e4d-9462-e0eb6c40068c" :step/math-academy-id 38130 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093205}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093205 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092316 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091927 :question/id #uuid "ae39bb45-ac10-562a-bac6-4f192c5f0aed" :question/math-academy-id "e-10111"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0ebe700-63af-57d4-ae8f-9e09dac5cf76" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092559} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092563} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275146}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Conditional Probability Using the Law of Total Probability"} :step/id #uuid "b7b7a4f5-8ab3-527e-b01f-844c9aeadcbe" :step/math-academy-id 38132} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093206 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093477 :tutorial/content "Suppose that a particular garden is divided into three small plots. There are $10$ roses in the first plot, of which $5$ are red, $12$ roses in the second plot, of which $6$ are red, and $14$ roses in the third plot, of which $4$ are red. What is the probability that a randomly selected rose from the garden is red?\n\nLet $A$ be the event that a chosen rose is red, and\n\n- let $B_1$ be the event that the selected rose comes from the first plot,\n- let $B_2$ be the event that the selected rose comes from the second plot,\n- let $B_3$ be the event that the selected rose comes from the third plot.\n\nNotice that the disjoint union\n\n$$\nB_1\\sqcup B_2\\sqcup B_3\n$$\n\ncovers the entire sample space, and that there are a total of\n\n$$\n10+12+14=36\n$$\n\nroses in the garden.\n\n- To calculate $P(A \\cap B_1)$, we take the number of red roses in the first plot $(5)$ and divide by the total number of roses $(36)$. So, we have\n$P(A \\cap B_1) = \\dfrac{5}{36}$.\n- To calculate $P(A \\cap B_2)$, we take the number of red roses in the second plot $(6)$ and divide by the total number of roses $(36)$. So, we have\n$P(A \\cap B_2) = \\dfrac{6}{36}$.\n- To calculate $P(A \\cap B_3)$, we take the number of red roses in the third plot $(4)$ and divide by the total number of roses $(36)$. So, we have\n$P(A \\cap B_3) = \\dfrac{4}{36}$.\n\nSo, to calculate the probability that a randomly chosen rose is red, we use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(A) &= P(A∩B_{1}) + P(A∩B_{2}) + P(A∩B_{3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{36} + \\frac{6}{36} + \\frac{4}{36} \\\\\n&= \\frac{15}{36} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{12}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- You may have noticed that there is an easier way to solve this problem. That is, to sum the total number of red roses $(5+6+4 = 15)$ and divide by the total number of roses $(10+12+14 = 36)$. However, you're encouraged to work with the law of total probability as it's essential to get used to the concept.\n- A little later in this lesson, we'll work with a version of the law of total probability that isn't intuitive. Understanding the law of total probability in its simplest form will help when things get trickier.\n\nLet's see another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "9b6238d0-0917-5d62-b878-311127fce7fe" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6446 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "1477f9c6-f997-5b76-9d95-747c31eb50a4" :step/math-academy-id 38958 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093202}}] :activity/title "Extending the Law of Total Probability" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186092453}]}
